+# Placement testing
+
+> Adaptive tests that estimate a student's CEFR level. Everything here is rule-based and deterministic — no AI, and no item-calibration data needed to start.
+
+[← Tests & Practice](Tests-and-Practice) · [Question types](Question-Types) · [Research Agenda](Research-Agenda)
+
+**Set up:** Assessments → Tests → Test Builder → **Mode: Placement**, then pick an engine.
+
+## Which engine?
+
+| Engine | You author | A student sees | Best when |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **Multistage (MST)** | A routing section, then easy / medium / hard sections | One *section* at a time | You already have a fixed set of sections |
+| **Staircase** | Sections tagged with a CEFR level (each is a pool of questions) | One *question* at a time, no going back | You have a pool of questions per level |
+| **Generator** | Only a CEFR range, skills and min/max question count | One question at a time, pulled live from your [question bank](Tests-and-Practice#question-bank) | You keep a tagged question bank |
+
+All three end with a **provisional CEFR estimate** that feeds the student's CEFR overview and learning path until a real graded assessment replaces it.
+
+## How the staircase works
+
+The staircase is deliberately simple, so you can explain it to a student or a parent:
+
+1. The student starts at **A2**.
+2. **Two correct answers in a row** at a level → move **up** one level.
+3. **One wrong answer** → move **down** one level.
+4. A **reversal** is when the direction changes (up-then-down or down-then-up).
+5. After **two reversals** the run has *converged* and stops. A safety cap ends a run after **12 questions** so it cannot go on forever.
+6. The estimate is the **level the student is standing on when the run ends**.
+
+**Worked example.** A2 ✓ ✓ → B1 ✓ ✗ → A2 *(reversal 1: up then down)* ✓ ✓ → B1 ✗ → A2 *(reversal 2)* → stop. Estimate: **A2**.
+
+Notes:
+- Reversals only count when the level actually changes (a miss at A1 can't move down, so it is not a reversal).
+- A teacher **nudge** during a live run moves the level up or down for the next question and resets the "correct in a row" counter.
+- The generator engine uses the same stepping rule, but within your chosen CEFR range, and has its own min/max question count instead of the 12-question cap.
+- The step sizes (start at A2, 2-up, 1-down, 2 reversals, cap 12) are **fixed**, not configurable. They come from a classic up-down method, but nobody has yet checked how well they fit language-learner populations — see the [Research Agenda](Research-Agenda).
+
+## How multistage routing works
+
+Each stage has a **cut-off percentage**. Scoring at or above it sends the student to the "pass" section, otherwise to the "fail" section. The estimate is the **highest CEFR-tagged section on the path that the student passed** (a terminal section with no routing uses a 60% default). If nothing was passed, the lowest level on the path is used, so a struggling student still gets a starting estimate.
+
+## Elo ratings (question ordering)
+
+Within a level, which question is asked first is decided by an **Elo rating** per question:
+
+- Every question starts at **1200** unless you set one.
+- Each level has a fixed anchor: A1 600 · A2 900 · B1 1200 · B2 1500 · C1 1800 · C2 2100 (each level covers ±150 around its anchor).
+- After a submission, every asked question's rating is nudged (K-factor 24): a correct answer lowers the item's rating (it was easier than expected), a miss raises it. The update runs on the server so simultaneous submissions don't overwrite each other.
+- Elo ratings are **teacher-only**; students never see them. They only affect **which item in a level comes next** — the staircase still decides the level.
+
+## How sure is the estimate?
+
+The live monitor and results show a **confidence badge** that reads the run itself:
+
+| Badge | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| Low | The student has only moved one way so far — the level is "where they were last pushed", not a bracketed level |
+| Medium | One reversal: the level is bracketed between two neighbours |
+| High | Two reversals: the run converged and the estimate stops moving |
+
+This badge describes how *settled* the run is. It is **not** a measured error or a validated accuracy figure. Treat a placement result as a starting point for a conversation, not a verdict. Collecting evidence on accuracy is on the [Research Agenda](Research-Agenda).
+
+## Rules enforced on the server
+
+When Supabase is connected, the server replays the student's path and rejects a forged or impossible one, and the generator picks questions on the server so the student's browser never receives the whole bank.
