index 0000000..9c3bcd2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Research-Agenda.md
@@ -0,0 +1,153 @@
+# Research Agenda — educational, didactic & pedagogic questions
+
+> The app makes many design choices (thresholds, step sizes, weights, scoring rules). This page lists, per area, **what the app does today** (checked against the code on 2026-11-19) and **what still needs to be researched or validated** before those choices can be called evidence-based.
+>
+> Citations named here are **starting points to look up and verify**, not settled facts. Mark an item ✅ when it has been researched and the answer is written down somewhere teachers can find.
+
+**Priority:** 🔴 affects marks or placement decisions directly · 🟠 affects teacher judgement or feedback · 🟡 nice to know
+
+**Two ways to answer a question:** *desk research* (literature, exam-board and curriculum documents) and *pilot data* (collect real results from your own classes, then compare — see [Studies you can run](#studies-you-can-run-with-the-app)).
+
+---
+
+## 1. Placement tests & the staircase method
+
+How the staircase works is explained in [Placement testing](Placement-Testing). The open questions:
+
+| # | Question | What the app does today | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1.1 | **Is the staircase rule well-founded?** | Start A2; 2 correct → up one level; 1 miss → down one level; stop after 2 reversals or 12 questions. Fixed, not configurable. | 🔴 |
+| 1.2 | **What accuracy does a run converge to?** A 2-correct-up / 1-miss-down rule looks like Levitt's "transformed up-down" method, which settles near ~70.7% correct. Does that match "the level a student *has* mastered"? | Not stated anywhere in the app | 🔴 |
+| 1.3 | **Are 2 reversals and ≤ 12 items enough?** Staircase procedures in the literature usually average more reversals; what is the error of an estimate after so few items? | The confidence badge (low/medium/high) counts reversals only; it is not a measured error | 🔴 |
+| 1.4 | **How accurate is the estimate compared with a trusted measure?** (A full Cambridge/CEFR-aligned test, teacher judgement, or the student's later graded results.) Include test–retest stability. | Estimate is labelled *provisional*; no validation data exists | 🔴 |
+| 1.5 | **Does a wrong answer mean the student is below that level?** Slips, guessing (25% on 4-option MC), unfamiliar item formats and one-off fatigue all cause misses. Should a single miss move a student down? | One miss always moves down | 🔴 |
+| 1.6 | **Is A2 the right starting level** for every year and track (e.g. groep 8 vs jaar 5 VWO)? Should the start follow the class's expected range? | Fixed A2 for staircase; the generator starts mid-range | 🟠 |
+| 1.7 | **Are the CEFR-tagged question pools actually at the level they claim?** Who tags them, with what evidence (descriptor match, word-list profile, pre-testing)? | Teacher tags the level; a passage level check exists for text vocabulary only | 🔴 |
+| 1.8 | **Elo for item difficulty with small classes.** Elo ordering starts at 1200 and moves by K=24 per response; how many responses before a rating is trustworthy? Does it drift when the student mix changes? Compare with Rasch/IRT as the "proper" approach. (See Pelánek's review of Elo in educational systems.) | Fixed level anchors (A1 600 … C2 2100), K=24, atomic update after each submission | 🟠 |
+| 1.9 | **Multistage (MST) cut-offs.** What routing threshold (percent correct) is appropriate for easy/medium/hard stages? How many items per stage give acceptable misrouting rates? | Teacher-set cut-off per section; 60% default for a terminal section | 🔴 |
+| 1.10 | **Mixing question types in one pool.** Do production items (typed) and recognition items (MC) land on the same CEFR scale? | Any scorable type can be in a pool | 🟠 |
+| 1.11 | **Skill coverage.** A single adaptive run mixes reading, grammar and listening; is one overall level meaningful, or should each skill be estimated separately? | Generator can filter by skill; the estimate is one level | 🟠 |
+| 1.12 | **Accommodations.** Do read-aloud, extra time or the no-going-back rule change what the placement measures? | No going back in staircase/generator runs; read-aloud available | 🟡 |
+
+**Suggested sources:** Levitt (1971) on transformed up-down methods; Council of Europe, *CEFR Companion Volume* (2020); research on computerised-adaptive and multistage testing (e.g. Wainer); Pelánek (2016) on Elo in adaptive learning; the technical manuals of established online placement tests.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Grading with rubrics — proven practice
+
+| # | Question | What the app does today | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 2.1 | **What does the evidence say about rubric reliability and validity?** Analytic vs holistic vs single-point; how many criteria and levels are optimal? (Start with Jonsson & Svingby's 2007 review of rubric reliability, and Brookhart's work on rubrics.) | Supports all three modes; no guidance on counts | 🟠 |
+| 2.2 | **Weighted vs total points.** Do weights distort or improve fairness? How to set weights defensibly? | Total, weighted (%) and single-point modes | 🟠 |
+| 2.3 | **Converting a rubric score into a mark.** Linear percentage vs "cut score" scales; what is defensible for Dutch VO (1–10 scale, N-term)? | Grade scales with percentage bands per letter/mark | 🔴 |
+| 2.4 | **Inter-rater reliability and calibration.** How much do two teachers differ with the same rubric? Is the default **moderation dispute threshold** sensible? Does comparative grading actually improve consistency? (Look at comparative-judgement research.) | Second marking, moderation queue with a configurable point threshold, comparative (side-by-side) grading | 🔴 |
+| 2.5 | **Effective feedback.** What kinds of written/voice feedback improve later work (e.g. Hattie & Timperley; Black & Wiliam on formative assessment)? Does a comment bank encourage generic feedback? | Comment bank, voice feedback, read-aloud of feedback | 🟠 |
+| 2.6 | **Late penalties and score modifiers.** Which are fair and motivating? Does the evidence support deducting for lateness at all? | Free-form percentage/point/level modifiers with a reason | 🟠 |
+| 2.7 | **Group grading.** How to be fair to individuals in a shared product? | Fan-out of group scores, with per-criterion exclusion for individual parts | 🟠 |
+| 2.8 | **Self- and peer assessment accuracy.** How closely do students' ratings track the teacher's, and under what training? (Panadero & Jonsson on rubrics and self-regulation.) | Self-scores and peer scores stored beside teacher scores; peer analytics show leniency and consistency | 🟠 |
+| 2.9 | **Do rubrics shown to students before the task improve performance?** | Preview links and student feedback pages exist | 🟡 |
+| 2.10 | **Bias.** Halo effect, order effects (grading the 30th paper), name/handwriting bias. Does anonymised grading help? | No blind-grading mode (names can only be hidden in the live monitor) | 🟠 |
+
+---
+
+## 3. Question types & scoring rules
+
+| # | Question | What the app does today | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 3.1 | **Partial credit for "select all" and hot-text.** The score is the share of options judged correctly *including options correctly left unselected*, so a blank answer can score above zero. Is that fair, or should it be (correct − incorrect) / total, or all-or-nothing? | Partial credit on by default for [Multiple-response](Question-Type-Multiple-response) and [Hot text](Question-Type-Hot-text) | 🔴 |
+| 3.2 | **Guessing.** Should MC/True-False apply a correction for guessing or just have more items? | No guessing correction | 🟠 |
+| 3.3 | **How tolerant should typed answers be?** When do spelling slips mask what you want to measure? | Four opt-in tolerances; near-misses flagged for human review | 🟠 |
+| 3.4 | **Dictation scoring.** Word-level edit distance weighs every word equally; do character-level or error-type schemes track listening and spelling skill better? | Word-level alignment, no speech recognition | 🟡 |
+| 3.5 | **Key word transformation.** Marks per part and word limits follow the exam format; how well does automatic marking agree with trained markers? | Each `//` part = one mark, strict word limit, key word must appear | 🔴 |
+| 3.6 | **Cloze design.** What does each gap type measure — rational deletion, fixed-ratio, C-test? Which is valid for which CEFR level? | Several generators, teacher reviews | 🟠 |
+| 3.7 | **Ordering and matching.** Position-based scoring penalises one early slip heavily; alternatives? | Position-by-position scoring | 🟡 |
+| 3.8 | **Auto-mark vs human mark agreement.** Run a sample of short-answer and key-word items through both. | Auto-scores can be confirmed in one click when "confident" | 🔴 |
+| 3.9 | **Item analysis thresholds.** What discrimination index and distractor-pull values should make a teacher revise or drop an item? | Discrimination (upper/lower split) and distractor pull shown, no recommended cut-offs | 🟠 |
+| 3.10 | **Listening play limits.** Are limits enforced only in the browser a meaningful exam condition? | Play counter, logged, browser-enforced | 🟡 |
+| 3.11 | **Class-average adjustment ("curve").** When is curving defensible? | Reversible, audited adjustment | 🟠 |
+
+---
+
+## 4. CEFR levels, mastery and learning paths
+
+| # | Question | What the app does today | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 4.1 | **Source of the expected CEFR ranges per school year and VO track.** The code describes them as coming from "the school's curriculum benchmark table". Are they backed by SLO, the Dutch reference frameworks or exam-board data? | A fixed table per (year, track) | 🔴 |
+| 4.2 | **When has a student "achieved" a level?** | Highest achieved level per skill, or the highest attempted level shown as "working toward" | 🔴 |
+| 4.3 | **Calibrating expectations from the cohort.** After ≥10 assessed students, a majority on one side can move the expectation by one sub-level. Is that statistically sound for small classes? | Gated ±1 sub-level calibration; the standard range always stays visible | 🟠 |
+| 4.4 | **Grammar mastery blend.** Writing 45%, tests 35%, flashcards 20% until at least 40 fully-observed items exist (fully data-driven at 120). What is the evidence for those weights and that sample size? | Deterministic fit, blended in gradually | 🟠 |
+| 4.5 | **Vocabulary profile validity.** The 64k-word CEFR index is built from open word lists (see `docs/VOCAB_WORDLISTS_PROVENANCE.md`). How well do the word levels agree with published lists (English Vocabulary Profile, CEFR-J)? How reliable are *reading-level estimates* from a short text? | Rule-based; indicator only | 🟠 |
+| 4.6 | **Readability formulas for learners.** Flesch-Kincaid was designed for native readers; is it meaningful for L2 texts? | Shown in the writing statistics | 🟡 |
+| 4.7 | **Grammar range detection (CEFR-J levels from a dependency parse).** How accurate is the parser on learner English, which has errors? | Shown as an indicator, "not a grade" | 🟠 |
+| 4.8 | **Learning-path triggers.** Three consecutive low scores, or trailing the class average, trigger recommendations. What false-positive rate is acceptable? Do such alerts change teacher actions or outcomes? | Rule-based, no AI | 🟠 |
+| 4.9 | **Writing-trend flag.** "Weak" = last three essays all ≤ 60%. Evidence for the threshold and window? | Fixed rule | 🟡 |
+| 4.10 | **Statistics insights.** Flags at class average < 55%, a criterion ≥ 15 points below average, or an inter-class gap ≥ 20 points. Where do these cut-offs come from? | Hard-coded thresholds | 🟡 |
+| 4.11 | **Language portfolio and self-assessment.** How reliable are learners' CEFR can-do self-ratings at different ages? | Self-assessment against can-do statements | 🟡 |
+
+---
+
+## 5. Flashcards & spaced repetition
+
+| # | Question | What the app does today | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 5.1 | **FSRS in classrooms.** FSRS (the algorithm behind modern Anki, evaluated by Ye et al.) was tuned on adult self-study data. How does it behave with teenagers, with short term-time reviewing, and with fixed deadlines? | `ts-fsrs` with default settings | 🟠 |
+| 5.2 | **Desired retention.** What target (e.g. 90%) and daily review load suits a class? | Default parameters | 🟠 |
+| 5.3 | **Card design.** Single words vs sentences vs cloze; production vs recognition; how many new cards per day? | Front/back, optional phonetic and part of speech | 🟡 |
+| 5.4 | **Pre-teaching decks from a text.** Does pre-teaching the words above a student's level actually improve comprehension? | Words above target level seed a deck | 🟠 |
+| 5.5 | **Quizzing from the deck.** Testing effect vs. a deck that has been seen: does generated testing add learning value? | Generate questions from a deck | 🟡 |
+
+---
+
+## 6. Writing, live writing and peer review
+
+| # | Question | What the app does today | Priority |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 6.1 | **Timed vs process writing.** What does a 5–15 minute sprint tell you about writing ability versus a drafted essay? | [Live Writing Sessions](Live-Writing-Sessions) | 🟠 |
+| 6.2 | **Vocabulary-based early level indicator.** How well does word-list profile predict a writing CEFR level for short texts? | Indicator only, never graded | 🟠 |
+| 6.3 | **Peer voting and anonymous reading.** Effects on motivation and on weaker writers? Risks of popularity voting or embarrassment? | One vote per pair, teacher can reveal or redistribute | 🟠 |
+| 6.4 | **Self-evaluation prompts.** Do three-option emoji self-checks produce usable information? | Included in the loop | 🟡 |
+| 6.5 | **Leaderboards and live word counts.** Motivation versus anxiety and quantity-over-quality. | Leaderboard is a teacher toggle | 🟠 |
+| 6.6 | **Typed vs pasted text signals** in monitoring: how reliable are they as integrity evidence, and how should teachers use them? | Shown to the teacher | 🟠 |
+
+---
+
+## 7. Fairness, accommodations and integrity
+
+| # | Question | Priority |
+|---|---|---|
+| 7.1 | Does the **read-aloud accommodation** change what a reading test measures? When is it appropriate for graded tests? | 🟠 |
+| 7.2 | Are **proctoring signals** (tab switches, copy/paste, battery) valid indicators of cheating, or do they penalise students with unreliable devices? Safe Exam Browser support here is advisory only. | 🔴 |
+| 7.3 | **Accessibility and dyslexia** — what do evidence-based reading supports look like for test pages and feedback? | 🟡 |
+| 7.4 | **Bias in item content and in auto-marking** across first-language groups. | 🟠 |
+| 7.5 | **Data protection of learning data** — lawful basis, retention periods (the app anonymises after a school-set number of years), minors' data. | 🟠 |
+
+---
+
+## 8. Practice with teachers
+
+| # | Question | Priority |
+|---|---|---|
+| 8.1 | How much **time** does the app save on grading and feedback, and where does it cost time (e.g. setting up rubrics, tagging bank questions)? | 🟠 |
+| 8.2 | Which **dashboards and alerts** do teachers act on, and which are ignored? | 🟠 |
+| 8.3 | Which **docs and help** do teachers actually read; where do they get stuck in first use? | 🟡 |
+| 8.4 | Do teachers **trust** provisional placement levels? What wording prevents over-reading a provisional estimate? | 🔴 |
+
+---
+
+## Studies you can run with the app
+
+1. **Placement validation.** Have 60+ students take a placement test and a trusted reference (for example a full level test or two teachers' independent judgement). Compare levels, report exact and ±1-level agreement and repeat after two weeks for test–retest.
+2. **Staircase rule experiment.** Replay recorded `levelPath` traces under alternative rules (3-correct-up, 2-miss-down, 4 reversals) and compare the final level with the reference.
+3. **Rater agreement.** Two teachers mark the same 20 essays with the same rubric; compute agreement per criterion, then repeat with comparative grading.
+4. **Auto-mark audit.** For 200 short-answer and key-word responses, compare auto-score with a teacher's mark; list the disagreements.
+5. **Item review.** After a class test, use item analysis to find items with low discrimination and read them for ambiguity.
+6. **Flashcard trial.** Two parallel classes use decks with different retention settings; compare delayed vocabulary tests.
+
+Because everything in the app is deterministic and stored, these studies can reuse the real answer traces (always handled under your school's data-protection rules).
+
+---
+
+## Where the app already documents its rules
+
+- [Placement testing](Placement-Testing) · [Question types](Question-Types) · [CEFR, mastery & learning paths](CEFR-and-Learning-Paths)
+- `docs/VOCAB_WORDLISTS_PROVENANCE.md` in the repository: where the vocabulary lists come from
